{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And the chief baker saw that it was good.</b> Meaning — he who had dreamed saw that the interpretation of the chief cupbearer was good, meaning: correct, and he himself was a witness who saw it, unlike the chief cupbearer, who perhaps would not validate it as certainly being this interpretation." "<b>Or</b> it may further intend in this manner: <b>that it was good</b> he spoke to him regarding his interpretation, as though he said, for he interpreted good and not evil, and he thought that perhaps him too he would foretell good tidings. But his hope was a crushing of spirit, for they showed him the exile of Israel, that the nations would devour them — nations likened to birds, as stated (Genesis 15:11, and Targum Yonatan there): ״וַיֵּרֶד הָעַיִט עַל הַפְּגָרִים״ — Israel, who are alluded to as bread, as it is written (Psalms 14:4): ״אֹכְלֵי עַמִּי אָכְלוּ לֶחֶם״. He also alluded by saying <b>on my head</b>, that Israel are supreme above all the nations. And you will find that in the dream of the chief cupbearer, he did not mention that Pharaoh drank from the cup, but only its being given into his hand — for honor and elevation — whereas in the dream of the chief baker, he told of the birds eating from the basket."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:21.274080368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}